H2: Frequently asked questions
P: FixMyCar says the Mazda 2 has a decent, if not outstanding, reliability record and that there are no universally agreed years to avoid. Carbuyer&#39;s guide to the 2007-2015 car says it has an excellent reputation for reliability. What Car? gives the 2015-2025 Mazda 2 a reliability rating of 96.4%. FixMyCar says the 2019-present car has very few common issues.
P: Air conditioning comes up in three of the sources. FixMyCar says the system is problematic in both the second and third generations, Auto Express calls faulty air-con one of the more common gripes, and the RAC says a few issues have been raised on the 2015-2019 car. FixMyCar also mentions the odd ABS sensor failure and carbon build-up, and says diesels can suffer a blocked diesel particulate filter if used for many short journeys.
P: Yes. DVSA&#39;s recall data lists several Mazda2 recalls. They include a low-pressure fuel pump impeller that may become deformed, passenger airbag inflators that may not operate as intended, an engine protection logic that does not work properly, and a front shock absorber lower nut and bolt that may not have been tightened to specification. Enter the registration in our [recall check](/car-recall-check) to see whether a particular car has work outstanding.
P: Run the air conditioning and check that it blows ice cold. Check the clutch for slippage or a notchy feel, and look over the alloy wheels for parking scuffs. If you are looking at a diesel, ask how the car was used, because short trips are known to clog the diesel particulate filter. Check that any recall work has been done.
