H2: Known Faults - Mazda MX-5
H3: Rust on the Mk1
P: HIGH ú Any miles ú Get a quote
P: Octane's buying guide to the 1989-1998 Mk1 says rust is the single biggest issue facing any Mk1 MX-5. It says to check that the hood drain tubes ahead of the rear wheels are clear, because blockages allow water to collect inside the sills, often leading to extensive structural corrosion. According to evo, the top rot-spots are the inner and outer rear wheelarches, the lower rear wings where they go over the sill (an MOT failure if bad), the base of the windscreen pillars and the lower edges of the front wings. Classic &#x26; Sports Car says that keeping the sill drains clear keeps rot at bay, and that it is a sign of a careful owner.
H3: Rust on the Mk2
P: HIGH ú Any miles ú Get a quote
P: Classic &#x26; Sports Car's Mk2 (1998-2005) buyer's guide says corrosion is the Mazda's biggest enemy and is always from the inside out. It suggests looking for examples with full history and evidence of caring owners who have kept the drain holes in the sills and doors clear.
H3: Rust on the Mk3
P: MONITOR ú Any miles ú Get a quote
P: Parkers, reviewing the Mk3 (2005-2015), says the car is incredibly good value as a used buy, though it shares the earlier generations' propensity to rust. It adds that rust is a serious foe of older MX-5s and that it seems Mazda has not learned its lesson.
H3: Mk1 Short-Nose Crankshaft
P: MONITOR ú Any miles ú Get a quote
P: The magazine evo says early 1.6 Mk1 cars with a short-nose crankshaft can chew the end of the shaft and the keyway if the bottom pulley works loose. Hagerty UK says very early cars have these short-nose crankshafts, identified by four slots in the bottom pulley rather than eight.
H3: Seizing Brakes
P: MONITOR ú Any miles ú Get a quote
P: Autocar's Mk3 (2005-2015) buying guide says to brake hard to check for pulling, because the calipers are prone to seizing. Classic &#x26; Sports Car, writing about the Mk2 (1998-2005), says the handbrake is prone to seizing, especially on cars that are rarely used in the winter.
H3: Roof and Damp
P: CHECK ú Any miles ú Get a quote
P: Carbuyer's Mk3 guide says to check fabric roofs carefully for signs of damage, including the frame and rear window. Parkers says convertibles can suffer from damp and mustiness, especially if forgetful owners leave the roof open in the rain. For the folding hard-top Roadster Coupe, the RAC says the roof mechanism should work quickly with no need for manual alignment. On the 2005-2009 car, the RAC says to check the hood for signs of rips, damage or discolouration. For the Mk4, What Car? says that if you want to check the roof for leaks, you can head to an automatic car wash and put the car through a cycle.
H3: Mk4 Gearbox and Electrics
P: MONITOR ú Any miles ú Get a quote
P: The RAC, reviewing the Mk4 (2015-2022), says it has heard of gearbox issues, with problems in gear selection from 1st to 2nd and from 2nd to 3rd. What Car? says most Mk4 owners reported few issues, with many praising the car's dependable performance and minimal need for repairs. It adds that a few mentioned occasional electrical problems, particularly with the infotainment system, including unresponsive screens and connectivity issues.
H3: Engines and Gearboxes
P: MINOR ú Any miles ú Get a quote
P: What Car? says the word normally used for the Mk3's 1.8 and 2.0-litre engines is bullet-proof, so as long as they have been regularly maintained and the service history tallies up, you should experience few problems. It says the engines and gearboxes of all MX-5s are generally strong, with few consistently reported problems, even in cars with above-average mileages. Autocar calls the Mk3 engine mostly bulletproof but says it is vital to keep the oil level at its correct level, so check that first. It adds that the action on the five-speed and six-speed gearboxes should be stiff from cold but loosen up.
H3: Alloy Lacquer, Tyres and Age
P: MINOR ú Any miles ú Get a quote
P: Carbuyer says the Mk3's alloy wheels can suffer from peeling lacquer, and suggests negotiating a discount if you spot this flaw. The RAC's 2005-2009 review says to check the alloys for kerbing damage and the tyres for wear. Carbuyer says MX-5s are known for their brilliant reliability, so most gremlins are minor and rarely crop up. Its page on the Mk3 (2005-2015) adds that its Driver Power 2014 owner satisfaction survey made it clear that age is catching up with the car. The RAC's review of the 2009-2015 car reports that a What Car? annual reliability survey confirmed the roadster to be the most reliable car in Britain.
H3: Safety Recalls
P: CHECK ú Any miles ú Ask a Mazda dealer
P: DVSA's recall data lists several MX-5 recalls, including these.
LI: The brackets used to secure the fuel pipes to the engine may not be tightened sufficiently.
LI: The impeller in the low-pressure fuel pump may become deformed, causing fuel pump failure.
LI: The metal bracket used to secure an under shield may separate from the vehicle.
LI: The automatic transmission may unexpectedly downshift.
LI: The control logic which monitors the charging control program fails to divert the electrical power away from the capacitor when a fault is detected within the capacitor circuit.
LI: Repeated thermal expansion and shrinkage in use may cause the front fog light socket holder to become detached from the fog light case.
LI: The floor mat fitted in the driver's footwell may interfere with the throttle pedal and cause the throttle to stick open.
P: Enter the registration in our recall check to see whether a particular car has work outstanding. For engines, versions and job prices, see our Mazda MX-5 parts guide .
H2: Breakdown cover
P: If a fault stops the car on the road, breakdown cover sends help to you. Compare two UK providers and check what each policy includes.
P: We earn a commission if you buy cover through these links, at no cost to you ( how we're paid ).
H2: Mazda MX-5 MOT results in 2025
P: There were 90,784 MOT tests of the Mazda MX-5 in 2025. 25.1% failed first time, against 27.7% for all cars. Older cars fail more often, so the fairer comparison is by age:
H3: What they failed on
LI: Suspension : 9.0% of tests (all cars 9.0%). Shock absorber prices
LI: Lamps, reflectors and electrical equipment : 8.0% of tests (all cars 10.6%). Headlight bulb prices by type
LI: Brakes : 7.2% of tests (all cars 6.2%). Brake pad prices , brake disc prices
LI: Noise, emissions and leaks : 4.7% of tests
LI: Visibility : 3.9% of tests (all cars 4.3%). Wiper blade prices
P: Initial MOT tests in Great Britain, 2025, from DVSA's anonymised MOT data; fail rate as DVSA calculates it (fails plus faults fixed during the test). Results cover every car recorded under the model name, so the older age bands can include earlier generations of it. Contains public sector information licensed under the Open Government Licence v3.0. MOT pass rates for every model and how they're worked out .
